/* QA40x sine -> THD: play 1 kHz into the mock's loopback and read the FFT's
   own displayed numbers back.

   Ordering matters.  The generator starts first so the loopback already
   carries signal when the analyzer begins its first frame; starting the FFT
   first would spend that frame on silence and the first result - which
   averages=1 makes THE result - would be of nothing.

   The wait is named, so a mock that never delivers a frame says which
   measurement never arrived instead of only how long it waited.  It throws on
   timeout, which fails the run: a scenario that quietly carried on with no
   data would assert NaN against every tolerance and report a pile of red
   checks whose real cause was one missing frame.

   THE LEVEL CHECK is the one assertion here sensitive to the whole gain
   chain: the frequency is right whatever the ranges do, and THD is a RATIO
   that survives them, so without it a 42 dB input-range error would sail
   through green.  Its expected value of -7.75 dBFS looks surprising for a
   0.1 Vrms request, and it is worth knowing exactly why it is not.

   THREE real QA40x quirks stack up, and all three are documented:

   1. The input range label LIES, deliberately.  A QA "N dBV" INPUT range is
      really an N-dBFS Vpp-differential reference, so its true RMS full scale
      is about N-9 dB (Qa40xLevels; Qa40xProtocol.verboseInputLabel even
      renders it as 'N "dBV" real N dBFS or N-9 dBV' for the operator).
      OUTPUT ranges, by contrast, are genuine dBV.  At the default 0 "dBV"
      input range the real full scale is
        FS_in_rms = cal_adc_r * 10^(0/20) / (2*sqrt2) = 0.988553/2.828427
                  = 0.3495063 Vrms
      which is exactly the fsRight the run writes into the QA403 card.

   2. The analyzer is a BALANCED output driving a DIFFERENTIAL input, and the
      mock models that faithfully: a 0 dBV (1 Vrms) output presents +-2 V at
      the differential input.  So the input sees sqrt2 times the RMS the
      generator was asked for - this is device physics, not a gain error.

   3. The right channel's DAC cal factor is 10^(-0.11/20) = 0.987417 at the
      +18 dBV output row (code 3), and the loopback divides by it.

   Putting them together for the 0.1 Vrms this scenario requests:
        V_seen = 0.1 * sqrt2 / 0.987417            = 0.1432234 Vrms
        peak fraction = 0.1432234 / 0.3495063      = 0.409789
        dBFS = 20*log10(0.409789)                  = -7.75 dBFS
   The measured value is -7.7018 dBFS, 0.05 dB away - the residual is the
   remaining cal term - so the tolerance is 0.5 dB.

   Two independent things pin the pieces of this so a future failure lands
   where its cause is: FftDbFsConventionTest fixes the dBFS column as
   PEAK-referenced (an rms reference would move this by 3.01 dB), and
   Qa40xOutputScalingTest fixes the OUTPUT side as true dBV - a full-scale
   RMS request reaching exactly full-scale peak, no stray sqrt2. Both are
   device-free unit tests, so if either convention moves, it fails there in
   one line of arithmetic rather than here, looking like broken hardware.

   THD is the assertion that needs no pinned range at all, being a ratio of
   harmonics to fundamental.  The mock's loopback is mathematically clean, so
   with coherent averaging and a near bin-snapped tone the harmonics should
   sit far below -100 dB.  If this ever fails, investigate before loosening
   it. */
startGenerator();
startFft();
waitUntil("the first averaged FFT frame", () -> fftHasResult(), 30);
check("fund-freq", fftFundamentalHz(), 1000.0, 1.0);
check("fund-level-dbfs", fftFundamentalDbFs(), -7.75, 0.5);
checkDbBelow("thd", fftThdDb(), -100.0);
